From 8d0d3726049fd065ca7c5c19715bddfba67aab27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:22:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AudioEngine: read the Atmos objects and the dialnorm an audio
 stream says it has

The player information knew nothing of what an audio stream says of its
presentation. Read it from the demuxed packets, so that it is the same
whether the stream passes through or is decoded (FFmpeg's decoders give
only the profile: Atmos, DTS:X, nothing more), and give it with the
labels that the CoreELEC builds of avdvplus have for it:

- Player.Process(audio.object.count) and audio.bed.channels: the dynamic
  objects and the bed channels of Dolby Atmos, from the object audio
  metadata of E-AC-3 JOC (OAMD in its EMDF container, ETSI TS 103 420
  5.5.2, 5.5.3, 8.2; looked for about once a second, forgotten after four
  looks without it) and from the 16-channel presentation of TrueHD's
  major sync (checked by its checksum); audiobedlayout (Hades) their
  layout, as 7.1.2 or LFE;
- Player.Process(audio.dialnorm): AC-3 and E-AC-3 (ATSC A/52 5.4.2.8 and
  Annex E; code 0 is -31 dB, as FFmpeg's decoder applies it; frames must
  pass their CRC) and DTS (ETSI TS 102 114 table 5-20, and the Rev2
  auxiliary data chunk, which takes priority).

What a stream does not say is not made up: such a label is empty. Not
given, and why: TrueHD's dialogue norm (what its codes mean is not in any
document that could be read), and the bed and objects of DTS:X (its
object audio is not in ETSI TS 102 114; the "bed" those builds show is a
speaker layout). The 16-channel meaning of TrueHD and the bit order of
the flag arrays of program_assignment are taken as MediaInfo and avdvplus
read them, two independent readings that agree; no public specification
of the first was found.

AudioPresentation.h is a reader of its own, pure, with a host test. The
idea, and the names and meaning of the three labels, come from avdvplus
(their CoreELEC builds); none of their code is used (their dialnorm is
code - 31 dB, which A/52 and FFmpeg do not say).
